1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Chaos in Guadalajara, Mexico, when the city streets explode.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:13,000
An airplane crash outside of Paris that ranks as one of the worst in history.

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Two dams in Italy collapse, engulfing a village in a tidal wave of sludge.

4
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Students and teachers in a small Texas town are entombed in the rubble of their school.

5
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:33,340
An oil tanker runs aground off the coast of England and introduces the world to the devastation of the first super spill.

6
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:37,700
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:55,383 --> 00:01:01,280
On the morning of April 22, 1992, a series of explosions rocked the streets of Guadalajara.

8
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,879
I told my family, get out of here. Something happened.

9
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,320
I don't know what, but get out of here.

10
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:13,040
Starting just after 10 a.m. and lasting over four hours,

11
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:17,560
at least five separate explosions devastated nearly 20 blocks of the city.

12
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:24,900
We don't know what happened in the city, just what the smoke, what explosion,

13
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:31,520
what saw the fire and a lot of people there.

14
00:01:31,521 --> 00:01:39,440
News of the massive devastation traveled to Texas.

15
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:42,480
And on the request of the Mexican consulate there,

16
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,940
one Houston-based team was asked to visit the disaster site.

17
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:54,220
I put together a group of firefighters, some medical people, some people from public works.

18
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:59,960
Our mission was to see what was needed and also to determine what caused the explosion if we could.

19
00:01:59,961 --> 00:02:06,280
We figured we'll start at the outer perimeters where the devastation is and try to work ourselves to the epicenter.

20
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:14,640
We walk around a corner and there's this giant ditch about 15 feet deep and some areas even deeper.

21
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:22,880
And it was just mind-boggling to walk out into that area and see the first 10 feet of the houses gone.

22
00:02:22,881 --> 00:02:33,720
A jagged trench exacting the path of the main sewer line had devoured everything in its path, including cars, buses and pedestrians.

23
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:39,340
Adjacent homes and businesses were reduced to rubble.

24
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:41,920
The scene resembled a war zone.

25
00:02:42,540 --> 00:02:50,120
By official counts, there were 215 dead, 1,600 buildings destroyed and 1,500 people injured.

26
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:53,980
The massive explosion had originated in the sewer main,

27
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:55,840
buried underneath the city streets.

28
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:59,820
The explosion came through the pipe, came through the dirt,

29
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,160
went straight up, and then all the debris came back down.

30
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:06,460
It was something like I'd just never seen before.

31
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:10,600
Forty years in the fire department, I'd seen a lot,

32
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:13,160
never seen anything quite like that.

33
00:03:14,020 --> 00:03:20,000
Most of the people conclude that there were about 3,000 deaths.

34
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:24,680
but the official figures are around 200.

35
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,460
They started to remove the debris 24 hours after the shock,

36
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:34,960
so they had no way to separate bodies from debris.

37
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:38,320
Days before the catastrophe,

38
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:41,180
residents in and around Sector Reforma,

39
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:44,600
a working-class neighborhood located in southeastern Guadalajara,

40
00:03:44,601 --> 00:03:47,020
had lodged nearly 600 complaints

41
00:03:47,021 --> 00:03:50,060
of a strong gasoline odor emanating from the sewers.

42
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,220
There was alarm.

43
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:56,500
There was phone calls to the fire department the night before

44
00:03:56,501 --> 00:03:59,280
because of the strong smell of gasoline.

45
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:03,420
And there was a little investigation of what was going on,

46
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:05,460
and everyone was told to go back to bed.

47
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:08,920
And less than 12 hours later, it all blew up.

48
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:13,680
In the wake of the destruction,

49
00:04:14,220 --> 00:04:16,440
residents demanded answers to what went wrong

50
00:04:16,441 --> 00:04:18,660
and why the area had not been evacuated.

51
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:26,700
Government tried to find some answer to give to the people,

52
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:33,760
but they created a couple of theories about a vegetable oil company,

53
00:04:34,340 --> 00:04:37,140
and there was no believable answer.

54
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:40,860
Despite what they were being told,

55
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:44,800
it was obvious that the substance that caused the explosion was gasoline.

56
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:50,420
And in Mexico, all of the gasoline comes from one source.

57
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:55,060
Pemex is the only oil producer in Mexico.

58
00:04:55,800 --> 00:05:00,920
It's a monopoly, and they produce and sell every liter of gasoline.

59
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:08,280
Somehow, large quantities of gasoline had seeped into the sewer line, creating a massive pipe bomb.

60
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:13,220
Three years earlier, the subway was built a few blocks away.

61
00:05:13,221 --> 00:05:17,040
and it had to cross where the main sewer goes.

62
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:21,300
So they created a U-turn underneath the subway rails.

63
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:24,320
Much like a trap under a household sink,

64
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:28,020
which causes a water plug that prevents sewer gases from entering the house,

65
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:31,020
an airlock was formed in the sewer itself.

66
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:36,020
This gasoline is flowing through these sewer pipes underneath the city,

67
00:05:36,021 --> 00:05:40,860
and finally it reaches the right ignition point,

68
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:43,000
the vapor density in the air.

69
00:05:43,220 --> 00:05:52,560
and possibly someone throws a lighted, you know, like a cigarette butt that's burning out into a sewer,

70
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:54,980
and boom, off goes the explosion.

71
00:05:55,380 --> 00:06:02,180
It sends out a pressure wave that pushes the rest of the vapors and the liquid gas farther down through the sewer system.

72
00:06:02,181 --> 00:06:07,580
Maybe it's too rich for a few seconds, and then it'll finally reach the right ignition point,

73
00:06:07,780 --> 00:06:10,700
and the burning gasoline then ignites the next section.

74
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:13,060
It explodes, and it keeps moving and moving and moving.

75
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:17,360
The series of explosions did not go beyond the U-turn in the sewer pipe.

76
00:06:17,361 --> 00:06:21,780
If there was no airlock, the explosion could have gone farther.

77
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:29,140
Or there could have been no explosion, but just a huge fire inside the sewer.

78
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:33,940
But not an explosion, because the heated air could have flown.

79
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:38,760
There was no option, because there was this U-turn, this airlock.

80
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:41,700
With the damage assessed and cause determined,

81
00:06:42,220 --> 00:06:46,340
The team from Houston just needed to locate the origin of the gasoline in the sewer.

82
00:06:47,620 --> 00:06:52,160
We went back to the devastation area, and while we were there,

83
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:56,360
the chief saw a pickup truck with workers that were going to leave,

84
00:06:56,520 --> 00:07:01,420
and he told the officials, said, why don't we catch that truck and just go with those guys,

85
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:05,800
hoping that it would take us to this PEMEX plant that we heard people talking about.

86
00:07:05,801 --> 00:07:13,040
So we jumped in the back of that pickup truck with the workers and went on to the Pemex plant.

87
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:20,260
When we got out and started checking our equipment, that's when the hydrocarbon sniffers started going crazy.

88
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:23,040
And it started saying that explosion was imminent.

89
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:27,880
And the farther we went, the smell of the gasoline was just horrendous.

90
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:32,040
And you look at the ground and the ground looks wet, but it's not wet with water.

91
00:07:32,580 --> 00:07:34,080
The ground's wet with gasoline.

92
00:07:34,081 --> 00:07:41,120
The ground is completely permeated with gasoline that leaked up through the soil from the pipes that were underground.

93
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:44,860
And then we definitely knew we had found where the leak was.

94
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:50,600
Right in front of the plant was where the pipe ran over the sewer line.

95
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:54,360
And that pipe was leaking in the ground.

96
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:56,200
And the ground was saturated.

97
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:59,940
It was down at least eight feet solid with gasoline.

98
00:07:59,941 --> 00:08:07,100
We saw them actually taking out the pipe that was rusted and leaking and putting in, trying to put in a new pipe.

99
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:13,060
The useful life of underground steel pipes, even when coated and wrapped,

100
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:19,760
generally does not exceed 30 years without inspection, repair, or replacement.

101
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:25,760
The pipe was just really, really rusted and big holes in it.

102
00:08:25,761 --> 00:08:31,060
and the pipe they were going to replace it with wasn't that much better shape.

103
00:08:31,740 --> 00:08:35,400
They had whitewashed it, put some whitewash on it,

104
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:41,960
and I don't know if that was going to be just temporary or just what that was about.

105
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:46,340
We found the cause. We found the origin of the gas.

106
00:08:46,820 --> 00:08:51,180
We felt pretty good about accomplishing our mission and gave a report to the consulate.

107
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:54,740
With their mission accomplished, the team returned to Houston.

108
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:59,540
In Guadalajara, a separate investigation was already underway.

109
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:03,700
The president of Mexico came here and he said,

110
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:11,320
three days, 72 hours, and I want to know who is the responsibility.

111
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:14,660
With a slightly different account than the Houston delegations,

112
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:20,020
the Mexican government's inquiry concluded that an iron water main had been illegally installed,

113
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:24,860
intersecting and establishing contact with the 12-inch steel pipeline.

114
00:09:24,861 --> 00:09:31,860
The dissimilar metal to metal contact in a moist environment caused corrosion to both lines.

115
00:09:31,861 --> 00:09:40,860
A half-inch hole was created in the gasoline pipeline, allowing an estimated 150,000 gallons of gasoline to leak into the soil.

116
00:09:40,861 --> 00:09:42,597
And a little hole.

117
00:09:42,598 --> 00:09:51,860
And from this little hole, flew or dumped all the thousands and thousands of liters of gasoline.

118
00:09:51,861 --> 00:09:56,414
gasoline. This is the official version of all the investigation.

119
00:09:56,415 --> 00:09:57,980
Upon completion of the

120
00:09:57,981 --> 00:10:03,800
investigation, the Mexican courts ruled that no one was at fault for the explosions and the case

121
00:10:03,801 --> 00:10:09,021
was unequivocally closed. The big question is could it happen again?

122
00:10:09,022 --> 00:10:10,940
When you're in the middle

123
00:10:10,941 --> 00:10:16,475
of the situation, you don't care about who did what or play a blame game.

124
00:10:16,476 --> 00:10:17,840
What we want to do

125
00:10:17,841 --> 00:10:22,980
is find out where's it coming from, why did it happen, is it going to happen again?

126
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:28,760
PEMEX offered unofficially help and money,

127
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:34,880
but they never accepted any of the responsibility of this explosion.

128
00:10:38,940 --> 00:10:43,320
After we came back, we had no idea what happened after that

129
00:10:43,321 --> 00:10:46,740
because it seemed like it was just, it dropped off into a black hole.

130
00:10:46,741 --> 00:10:51,860
Nobody really knew what was going on down there or like it had never happened.

131
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:55,300
But I just have this memory that I carry with me.

132
00:10:55,380 --> 00:10:56,200
I know what I saw.

133
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:57,700
I know what I experienced.

134
00:10:58,380 --> 00:11:00,020
And it wasn't good.

135
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:07,120
Remnants of the destruction and memorials to the victims of the 22nd of April

136
00:11:07,121 --> 00:11:10,740
serve as constant reminders of this massive tragedy

137
00:11:10,741 --> 00:11:15,740
that unfortunately is little known outside the streets of Guadalajara.

138
00:11:16,740 --> 00:11:27,420
When gasoline vapors ignite, one gallon of gasoline can explode with the same force as 14 sticks of dynamite.

139
00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:31,620
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

140
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:37,260
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

141
00:11:37,261 --> 00:11:48,760
McDonnell Douglas' mighty DC-10, built to compete against rivals Lockheed L-1011 and the Boeing 747,

142
00:11:49,460 --> 00:11:53,080
was one of the workhorses of the world's commercial jet fleet for 20 years.

143
00:11:53,820 --> 00:11:58,360
But in the early 1970s, the new wide-body design of the DC-10

144
00:11:58,361 --> 00:12:04,300
turned what could have been a survivable malfunction in a smaller plane into a deadly engineering problem.

145
00:12:05,180 --> 00:12:09,820
The cargo door could appear to be closed and latched, when in fact it was not.

146
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:15,000
The Federal Aviation Administration determined that this unsafe condition

147
00:12:15,001 --> 00:12:19,020
resulted in one of the worst aviation disasters in history

148
00:12:19,021 --> 00:12:23,900
and began the negative public perception of the DC-10 that it would never escape.

149
00:12:24,620 --> 00:12:27,440
The DC-10 was really got a bad rap.

150
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:29,960
I mean, it was the Corvair of the airplane industry.

151
00:12:30,780 --> 00:12:35,740
The DC-10 was the Douglas Aircraft Company's first and only wide-bodied commercial airliner.

152
00:12:36,620 --> 00:12:40,980
The jet featured a spacious cabin design with two aisles and three rows of seats.

153
00:12:41,740 --> 00:12:47,180
But the air pressurization systems for the enormous cabin were similar to those used in smaller planes.

154
00:12:47,181 --> 00:12:56,480
A lot of the design features of the wide-bodied aircraft were just scaled up from the previous narrow-bodied airplanes.

155
00:12:57,280 --> 00:12:59,640
The DC-10 was a short-range jumbo jet.

156
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:02,560
capable of carrying 343 passengers.

157
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:05,120
It was less expensive than its rivals

158
00:13:05,121 --> 00:13:08,420
and soon became the nation's most popular wide-body aircraft.

159
00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:13,340
But in 1972, less than two years after its introduction,

160
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:16,380
a DC-10 flying over Windsor, Canada,

161
00:13:16,760 --> 00:13:19,100
exhibited the first signs of problems in the new jet.

162
00:13:19,101 --> 00:13:23,160
There was an incident involving a DC-10 departing Detroit.

163
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:26,180
As it was climbing, the cargo door blew off

164
00:13:26,181 --> 00:13:28,100
and some cargo went out of the aircraft.

165
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:31,320
The aircraft had considerable control difficulties

166
00:13:31,321 --> 00:13:34,460
because the loss of the door, depressurization,

167
00:13:34,620 --> 00:13:36,700
had collapsed the floor and the back of the airplane.

168
00:13:37,060 --> 00:13:38,960
As the cargo door came off,

169
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:41,980
there was an explosive decompression of air in the cabin,

170
00:13:42,240 --> 00:13:43,280
escaping out of the hole.

171
00:13:44,140 --> 00:13:46,720
The pressure of the massive volume of air was so heavy

172
00:13:46,721 --> 00:13:48,720
that the cabin floor gave way.

173
00:13:49,820 --> 00:13:52,920
Even though control cables running under the cabin floor were damaged,

174
00:13:53,260 --> 00:13:56,460
the pilots were able to land the plane, and no life was lost.

175
00:13:57,340 --> 00:14:04,060
It was later determined that the vent door, a secondary locking device on the cargo door, wasn't secured before takeoff.

176
00:14:04,560 --> 00:14:09,400
When we got the report from the baggage handler, he said he was having a hard time closing the vent door.

177
00:14:09,620 --> 00:14:13,520
So he had to put his knee on it to close the door.

178
00:14:13,620 --> 00:14:20,560
And he forced and bent the mechanism such that the light went off in the cockpit and the vent door closed.

179
00:14:20,561 --> 00:14:27,020
So the cargo door apparently looked like it was sealed, and the door itself was closed, but it wasn't properly latched.

180
00:14:27,820 --> 00:14:32,780
The torque tube in the locking mechanism of the cargo door could be bent by human force.

181
00:14:33,700 --> 00:14:38,380
Once bent, the tube would allow the door to close, even though it was not properly latched.

182
00:14:38,381 --> 00:14:43,700
The NTSB wrote a report and issued safety recommendations to the FAA,

183
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:53,980
requesting that the FAA consider certain modifications to the door and to the aircraft to prevent the damage from a loss of the door.

184
00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:59,000
The FAA allowed for the modifications to be carried out on a voluntary basis.

185
00:14:59,580 --> 00:15:05,660
Although most carriers repaired their aircraft, some, especially those operating outside the U.S., did not.

186
00:15:05,661 --> 00:15:14,680
on march 3rd 1974 a turkish airways tc-10 departed from paris the first few minutes of the flight

187
00:15:14,681 --> 00:15:21,940
were routine but suddenly as the plane reached 13 000 feet the cargo door blew out the immense

188
00:15:21,941 --> 00:15:26,500
force of the pressurized air in the cabin as it rushed out the cargo door buckled the floor

189
00:15:26,501 --> 00:15:30,860
when the floor collapsed some of the seats actually detached from the floor and went out the door

190
00:15:30,861 --> 00:15:31,760
out the airplane.

191
00:15:32,320 --> 00:15:33,980
Vital control lines for the plane

192
00:15:33,981 --> 00:15:35,640
ran just under the cabin floor.

193
00:15:36,120 --> 00:15:37,420
They were instantly severed.

194
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:40,860
What happened is that the airplane,

195
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:43,700
because the floor collapsed

196
00:15:43,701 --> 00:15:47,800
and actually moved the controls mechanically,

197
00:15:48,100 --> 00:15:51,340
the crew could not overcome that mechanical difference.

198
00:15:51,500 --> 00:15:52,800
So the airplane came down.

199
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:55,760
The plane slammed into trees in a forest.

200
00:15:56,100 --> 00:15:58,540
All 346 passengers were killed.

201
00:15:58,541 --> 00:16:04,360
investigators soon discovered that the cause of the crash was almost identical to the accident

202
00:16:04,361 --> 00:16:10,400
in windsor according to the french report the door was not closed properly that's why it came open

203
00:16:10,401 --> 00:16:18,940
and they could not find evidence of the fixes that we had put out after the windsor accident

204
00:16:18,941 --> 00:16:26,020
in the wreckage in both cases the ground personnel forced the door or forced the handle

205
00:16:26,021 --> 00:16:30,660
and because there was a bending and compliance in the push rods,

206
00:16:31,420 --> 00:16:33,520
the handle actually closed,

207
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:38,900
therefore indicating that the cargo door was closed and latched.

208
00:16:39,480 --> 00:16:41,600
After the Turkish Airways disaster,

209
00:16:42,240 --> 00:16:47,840
the FAA mandated that the cargo door on DC-10s operating in the U.S. be modified.

210
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:52,600
There were several design changes made to ensure

211
00:16:52,601 --> 00:16:59,020
the operator of the door, could not close the vent door unless the latches were in the proper position.

212
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:03,620
As a result of the Paris accident, there was a fix put on the door,

213
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:06,760
which included what they called a feedback loop.

214
00:17:07,080 --> 00:17:13,560
Not only was there a rod going from the handle to the latch pins and to the small pressurization door,

215
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:15,000
there was a feedback loop.

216
00:17:15,001 --> 00:17:22,920
Another set of rods were connected back to the handle to show that the latch pins had in fact engaged properly.

217
00:17:24,040 --> 00:17:30,700
The crash also resulted in a mandated design change for not just the DC-10, but all wide-body aircraft.

218
00:17:31,360 --> 00:17:35,160
These planes had small vents between the cabin floor and the cargo area,

219
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:38,540
designed to equalize pressure between the two areas if needed.

220
00:17:39,420 --> 00:17:42,020
The venting system was adequate in smaller planes,

221
00:17:42,260 --> 00:17:45,240
but couldn't equalize the vast volume of air in a wide body.

222
00:17:45,500 --> 00:17:49,040
That design feature worked well on the narrow-bodied older aircraft,

223
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:52,060
and it was the same design used for the wide body.

224
00:17:52,560 --> 00:17:58,280
However, the scaling up led to the problem that this tremendous volume of air,

225
00:17:58,360 --> 00:18:03,500
now in a wide-bodied airplane, those traditional vents were not sufficient,

226
00:18:03,980 --> 00:18:07,320
and that's why we had the collapse of the floor in the two DC-10s.

227
00:18:07,321 --> 00:18:13,820
The fix entailed installing additional vents in the floor of the cabin, which would allow air to rapidly equalize.

228
00:18:13,821 --> 00:18:24,460
If you have a pressure change such as what would occur as a result of some, such as a cargo door opening up,

229
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:27,520
then these doors would open up.

230
00:18:27,680 --> 00:18:30,400
They're kind of spring-loaded and they basically open up.

231
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:36,120
And what they do is they allow then the pressure to vent around the outside of the floor

232
00:18:36,121 --> 00:18:38,700
and it keeps that whole floor from collapsing.

233
00:18:39,580 --> 00:18:43,200
A series of unrelated accidents involving DC-10s,

234
00:18:43,480 --> 00:18:47,080
including the 1979 crash of Flight 191 in Chicago,

235
00:18:47,540 --> 00:18:50,100
which resulted in the grounding of all DC-10s,

236
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:53,960
culminated with passengers refusing to fly on the aircraft.

237
00:18:55,340 --> 00:19:00,060
Orders for the plane slowed, and in 1989, the DC-10 went out of production.

238
00:19:00,620 --> 00:19:05,540
However, the existing 446 planes continued to operate around the globe.

239
00:19:05,541 --> 00:19:07,700
It is definitely a safe airplane.

240
00:19:07,701 --> 00:19:15,140
In fact, because of these accidents that it had, it was not only certified once after the Chicago accident,

241
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:20,820
there was an investigation of the accident, and there was an investigation of the design of the airplane.

242
00:19:20,920 --> 00:19:22,660
So it was really certified twice.

243
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:27,520
It had to be the safest airplane after all it went through of all time.

244
00:19:28,280 --> 00:19:32,880
Despite this troubled beginning, the DC-10 ultimately proved a reliable aircraft,

245
00:19:33,200 --> 00:19:36,080
and the safety record improved as the fleet hours increased.

246
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:44,180
In fact, the DC-10's lifetime safety record as of 2003 surpassed that of the venerable Boeing 747.

247
00:19:46,020 --> 00:19:50,100
Nevertheless, the remaining DC-10s operating in the United States,

248
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:55,060
once an icon of luxury travel, had been relegated to cargo duty.

249
00:19:55,061 --> 00:20:02,040
In 1979, DC-10s carried approximately 100,000 people daily

250
00:20:02,041 --> 00:20:06,460
on the 450 flights that departed each day in the U.S. alone.

251
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,680
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

252
00:20:11,780 --> 00:20:15,840
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

253
00:20:25,060 --> 00:20:32,440
At 12.22 p.m. on July 19, 1985, two mining dams burst in Stava, Italy.

254
00:20:34,120 --> 00:20:38,060
It was impossible to believe what we saw.

255
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:44,940
How two banks of sand could become two atomic bombs that caused the total destruction of the Stava Valley.

256
00:20:48,120 --> 00:20:52,620
The rupture unleashed a mud and sludge wave more than 100 feet wide.

257
00:20:52,621 --> 00:20:57,000
it flowed at a speed of 55 miles per hour

258
00:20:57,001 --> 00:20:59,240
it burst out

259
00:20:59,241 --> 00:21:01,100
and of course

260
00:21:01,101 --> 00:21:04,360
considering also the natural slope of this valley

261
00:21:04,361 --> 00:21:06,100
which is quite steep

262
00:21:06,101 --> 00:21:08,860
it gained progressive momentum

263
00:21:08,861 --> 00:21:11,220
it reached a very high acceleration

264
00:21:11,221 --> 00:21:13,000
in a matter of seconds

265
00:21:13,001 --> 00:21:16,420
the mudslide was over in four minutes

266
00:21:16,421 --> 00:21:18,020
but during that time

267
00:21:18,021 --> 00:21:20,380
268 people died

268
00:21:20,381 --> 00:21:23,380
and four hotels and 50 homes were flattened.

269
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:26,260
I saw that everything was swallowed up.

270
00:21:26,640 --> 00:21:29,560
There wasn't anything left, not houses, not anything.

271
00:21:30,540 --> 00:21:33,660
The town of Stava was virtually wiped off the map.

272
00:21:34,580 --> 00:21:38,180
The event has been called one of the worst industrial catastrophes in the world.

273
00:21:39,120 --> 00:21:40,700
But it could have been prevented.

274
00:21:42,840 --> 00:21:47,980
Stava is located in northeastern Italy, nestled in a picturesque Alpine valley.

275
00:21:47,981 --> 00:21:59,140
Though it's primarily a farming community, Stava has mountains rich in fluorite, and in 1934, a very productive mining industry began.

276
00:22:00,460 --> 00:22:04,240
In 1967, the first Tailings Dam was started.

277
00:22:04,680 --> 00:22:08,020
The Tailings Dam's purpose was to contain the waste material from the mine.

278
00:22:08,760 --> 00:22:11,400
The waste was a mixture of silt, sand, and water.

279
00:22:11,900 --> 00:22:16,860
The sand formed the dam structure, and the silt and water were stored in the basin itself.

280
00:22:17,980 --> 00:22:31,980
So their purpose is to store material at the fluid state first that couldn't be discharged directly into water courses because they've got a high pollutant content.

281
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:41,680
The silt was supposed to sink to the bottom of the basin and solidify, while the clear water was recycled into the streams and rivers of Stava.

282
00:22:42,220 --> 00:22:46,600
As more material was poured into the dam, the size of the basin increased.

283
00:22:47,200 --> 00:22:50,840
In just a few years, the tailings dam rose to 65 feet,

284
00:22:51,500 --> 00:22:53,500
twice the normal height for this kind of structure.

285
00:22:55,080 --> 00:23:00,880
In 1969, the mine owners decided to start another dam, right above the first one.

286
00:23:02,220 --> 00:23:04,560
The choice couldn't have been worse,

287
00:23:04,800 --> 00:23:08,080
because first of all we are on a steep slope,

288
00:23:08,320 --> 00:23:11,900
that these structures can be considered completely stable,

289
00:23:12,040 --> 00:23:16,040
only if they are built in the proper manner on flat ground.

290
00:23:16,041 --> 00:23:18,360
which of course was not the case here.

291
00:23:19,680 --> 00:23:21,700
Another factor that led to the disaster

292
00:23:21,701 --> 00:23:24,700
was the nature of the soil on which the dams were built.

293
00:23:26,120 --> 00:23:28,840
The marshy ground in Stava was not firm enough

294
00:23:28,841 --> 00:23:30,460
to hold the large earthen structures.

295
00:23:31,940 --> 00:23:34,100
Just walking along the slope,

296
00:23:34,101 --> 00:23:38,220
you see that the ground is soggy, it gives way,

297
00:23:38,620 --> 00:23:40,320
it's always saturated with water,

298
00:23:40,720 --> 00:23:45,060
and you can actually see springs coming out from the ground.

299
00:23:46,040 --> 00:23:51,740
Over the next six years, the two tailings' dams grew to more than 165 feet.

300
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:53,960
Local residents became alarmed.

301
00:23:54,660 --> 00:24:00,760
And in 1975, they called upon city authorities to inspect the dams and determine their stability.

302
00:24:01,840 --> 00:24:09,360
The thing was done by an engineer that was appointed by the company itself that ran the mine.

303
00:24:10,360 --> 00:24:17,060
So, of course, his judgment couldn't really be considered objective.

304
00:24:18,160 --> 00:24:20,960
This man, when he saw this structure, he says,

305
00:24:21,720 --> 00:24:24,160
it's a miracle that they haven't already collapsed.

306
00:24:25,460 --> 00:24:29,720
The engineer suggested that a test be done to determine the firmness of the soil.

307
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:33,720
The test would have cost approximately $13,000.

308
00:24:35,580 --> 00:24:39,020
And the direction of the mine said, that is too much money,

309
00:24:39,021 --> 00:24:40,085
just for this.

310
00:24:40,086 --> 00:24:45,640
We'll do it in a more simple way, that is getting an iron bar and pushing

311
00:24:45,641 --> 00:24:52,416
it through the soil and test like this manually if the soil was more or less firm.

312
00:24:52,417 --> 00:24:53,160
Of course

313
00:24:53,161 --> 00:24:55,599
a test like this is ridiculous.

314
00:24:55,600 --> 00:25:00,240
In fact, because of the marshy nature of the soil, the silt

315
00:25:00,241 --> 00:25:05,940
in the basins had not drained properly, becoming heavier and putting more pressure on the outer

316
00:25:05,941 --> 00:25:08,400
structure of the upper basin.

317
00:25:08,401 --> 00:25:13,320
On July 19th, 1985, the upper dam suddenly collapsed, down

318
00:25:13,321 --> 00:25:16,600
into the lower basin, which burst with an earth-shaking force.

319
00:25:23,300 --> 00:25:26,027
I saw the masses of sludge crashing down.

320
00:25:26,028 --> 00:25:29,220
From there, I went into a state of mental shock.

321
00:25:29,221 --> 00:25:33,760
My house wasn't there.

322
00:25:35,040 --> 00:25:36,740
I went just about out of my mind.

323
00:25:38,240 --> 00:25:41,240
My family was made up of my wife, so dear to me,

324
00:25:41,840 --> 00:25:43,520
three daughters and four sons.

325
00:25:43,900 --> 00:25:45,880
I was left with two daughters and a son.

326
00:25:47,700 --> 00:25:50,840
Rescue efforts continued for weeks after the Stava disaster.

327
00:25:51,780 --> 00:25:54,280
But very few victims were found alive.

328
00:25:57,160 --> 00:25:59,100
You couldn't go and help anybody.

329
00:25:59,220 --> 00:26:02,360
because there was so much money, it was like being in a bowl of pudding.

330
00:26:03,040 --> 00:26:05,560
If you saw arms, legs coming out of it,

331
00:26:05,920 --> 00:26:08,560
there was no way for you to get there to rescue these people.

332
00:26:09,360 --> 00:26:12,120
I remember that the first person I rescued was a child,

333
00:26:12,500 --> 00:26:13,800
who I remember to this day.

334
00:26:16,920 --> 00:26:18,620
In the official court proceedings,

335
00:26:19,340 --> 00:26:23,500
ten people involved in the construction, management and monitoring of the upper basin

336
00:26:23,501 --> 00:26:26,560
were found guilty of causing the disaster

337
00:26:26,561 --> 00:26:29,220
through negligence and incompetence.

338
00:26:30,040 --> 00:26:32,760
They were also convicted of multiple manslaughter charges.

339
00:26:33,780 --> 00:26:36,560
Previous owners and engineers were never arrested.

340
00:26:38,940 --> 00:26:40,720
I don't know what you call it in America.

341
00:26:41,040 --> 00:26:42,420
Indulgence is leniency.

342
00:26:43,700 --> 00:26:45,620
And they benefited from such leniency.

343
00:26:46,960 --> 00:26:51,180
The court-imposed fine needed $131 million,

344
00:26:52,080 --> 00:26:54,840
well over the $13,000 expense

345
00:26:54,841 --> 00:26:56,400
that might have prevented this disaster.

346
00:26:56,560 --> 00:26:58,500
ten years before.

347
00:27:00,360 --> 00:27:07,160
So greed, negligence, superficiality, speculation

348
00:27:07,161 --> 00:27:12,860
were the main aspects of behavior of the people

349
00:27:12,861 --> 00:27:15,120
that caused this disaster.

350
00:27:16,300 --> 00:27:22,500
Too many people think business is more important than life.

351
00:27:23,460 --> 00:27:26,600
The Italian Parliament also introduced a new law

352
00:27:26,601 --> 00:27:29,820
to make the construction and maintenance of tailings dams safer.

353
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:35,420
The entire country mourned the horrifying loss of the people of Stava.

354
00:27:37,460 --> 00:27:42,060
Pope John Paul II made a moving visit to the memorial site in 1998.

355
00:27:43,720 --> 00:27:45,720
In February 2002,

356
00:27:46,860 --> 00:27:51,040
several of the victims' families established the Stava 1985 Foundation.

357
00:27:52,500 --> 00:27:58,180
essentially so that the 268 people who died didn't die in vain.

358
00:28:00,180 --> 00:28:04,880
The town of Stava is now rebuilt, coming back to life after the disaster.

359
00:28:05,680 --> 00:28:11,080
It is a living, growing monument to those who were sacrificed by its destruction in 1985,

360
00:28:11,680 --> 00:28:16,860
and a lasting reminder of the resilience and spirit of those who survived.

361
00:28:16,861 --> 00:28:26,500
Since the Stava disaster, more than 35 other tailings dams around the world have failed in the same manner.

362
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:31,340
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

363
00:28:33,480 --> 00:28:37,620
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

364
00:28:39,740 --> 00:28:43,840
Natural gas is colorless, odorless, and tasteless.

365
00:28:43,841 --> 00:28:48,500
The familiar rotten egg smell we associate with gas is actually mercaptan.

366
00:28:49,140 --> 00:28:54,580
It's added before distribution, giving natural gas a distinct odor that serves as a safety device,

367
00:28:54,980 --> 00:28:57,000
allowing it to be detected when a leak occurs.

368
00:28:58,140 --> 00:29:02,080
Research to find an effective odorant began as early as the 1920s.

369
00:29:03,480 --> 00:29:07,080
However, it took one of the most heart-wrenching tragedies in American history,

370
00:29:07,081 --> 00:29:11,100
the deaths of over 300 school children

371
00:29:11,101 --> 00:29:13,980
to necessitate the use of a malodorous additive.

372
00:29:16,760 --> 00:29:18,760
By the early 1930s,

373
00:29:18,920 --> 00:29:21,220
the Great Depression meant economic disaster

374
00:29:21,221 --> 00:29:22,780
for the entire United States.

375
00:29:26,020 --> 00:29:28,280
But East Texas fared better.

376
00:29:30,840 --> 00:29:32,940
Dad Joyner came to East Texas,

377
00:29:33,220 --> 00:29:34,560
and he discovered the soil.

378
00:29:34,561 --> 00:29:43,180
And, of course, that changed the world of East Texas and this community forever.

379
00:29:43,560 --> 00:29:47,780
And this was the bright spot of the whole United States at that time.

380
00:29:49,380 --> 00:29:55,540
Like many oil boom towns, New London, Texas, just 130 miles east of Dallas,

381
00:29:55,960 --> 00:29:59,740
grew from approximately 850 to several thousand overnight.

382
00:29:59,741 --> 00:30:05,500
There were so many people moving in here with kids that the little two-room country school was too small.

383
00:30:06,200 --> 00:30:09,460
A modern school was built at a price of $300,000.

384
00:30:10,360 --> 00:30:14,700
At the time it was built, it was one of the richest school districts in the world.

385
00:30:15,600 --> 00:30:21,340
The state-of-the-art school sat on a 15-acre campus and had 10 oil wells located on its grounds.

386
00:30:26,997 --> 00:30:37,612
That was another exciting time of my life, moving from a three-room schoolhouse, where we had multiple grades in one room, moving to a large two-story brick school

387
00:30:37,613 --> 00:30:44,320
that had housed a high school, junior high, with all the facilities that any school in the country had.

388
00:30:45,320 --> 00:30:52,220
The school administration decided to save $300 a month on heating fuel by using natural gas.

389
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:57,620
At the time, natural gas was a worthless byproduct of petroleum extraction.

390
00:30:58,280 --> 00:31:01,680
Everybody got free gas from the oil companies.

391
00:31:01,840 --> 00:31:06,720
They had so much gas that they had to burn torches at night.

392
00:31:07,800 --> 00:31:10,480
A connection was made to the company's pipeline,

393
00:31:10,481 --> 00:31:15,320
and the free gas was fed directly into the existing pipelines in the school's sub-basement.

394
00:31:15,920 --> 00:31:20,040
But their connection leaked, and by March 18, 1937,

395
00:31:20,500 --> 00:31:25,900
nearly 6,000 cubic feet of gas had slowly collected in the school's poorly ventilated sub-basement.

396
00:31:27,420 --> 00:31:31,080
With no way of detecting the leak, the school day continued as usual,

397
00:31:31,440 --> 00:31:35,180
with a ticking time bomb just under the feet of London's students and teachers.

398
00:31:35,800 --> 00:31:39,680
Everything was normal until about 3.18 that afternoon.

399
00:31:39,681 --> 00:31:43,940
That was the last period of the day, and my last class was English class.

400
00:31:44,580 --> 00:31:51,200
At the beginning of the class, I asked a girlfriend that sat in front of me to change seats with me.

401
00:31:52,520 --> 00:31:57,320
I wanted to sit behind the little girl that sat in front of her.

402
00:31:58,120 --> 00:32:00,620
She graciously traded seats with me.

403
00:32:01,220 --> 00:32:08,640
Just minutes before the school was to be dismissed, the shop teacher plugged in a sander, and their esteemed school exploded.

404
00:32:09,680 --> 00:32:16,140
I didn't hear anything. Nothing. No sound at all.

405
00:32:16,800 --> 00:32:20,280
It must have been a horrible sound because I lost the hearing in my right ear.

406
00:32:20,860 --> 00:32:23,380
The ceiling fell in, and I don't know where it came from.

407
00:32:24,060 --> 00:32:28,120
Anyhow, that was all on top of me, and I had my face down against my desk,

408
00:32:28,540 --> 00:32:31,600
and this material was on top, and I couldn't move.

409
00:32:32,740 --> 00:32:38,460
And I heard some of the boys talking, and one of them said,

410
00:32:38,461 --> 00:32:41,180
there's somebody under here.

411
00:32:41,800 --> 00:32:43,280
And I said, I am.

412
00:32:43,460 --> 00:32:44,660
Get me out of here.

413
00:32:44,940 --> 00:32:47,940
So they lifted it up and helped me out.

414
00:32:48,700 --> 00:32:51,000
The impact of the combustion explosion

415
00:32:51,001 --> 00:32:52,900
had lifted the building into the air

416
00:32:52,901 --> 00:32:55,020
and it slammed back into the ground,

417
00:32:56,320 --> 00:32:57,700
entombing students and teachers

418
00:32:57,701 --> 00:32:59,480
in its brick, steel, and concrete.

419
00:33:00,680 --> 00:33:02,700
And I looked at the building

420
00:33:02,701 --> 00:33:05,160
and the front of the building was completely gone.

421
00:33:05,300 --> 00:33:08,220
It had blown up and just fell right back down

422
00:33:08,221 --> 00:33:09,620
on the ground flat.

423
00:33:10,100 --> 00:33:12,340
The devastating explosion was heard

424
00:33:12,341 --> 00:33:13,800
nearly four miles away.

425
00:33:15,080 --> 00:33:16,400
Within 15 minutes,

426
00:33:16,660 --> 00:33:18,340
the news had been relayed over telephone

427
00:33:18,341 --> 00:33:19,620
and Western Union lines.

428
00:33:20,740 --> 00:33:22,320
Frantic parents rushed to the school.

429
00:33:22,800 --> 00:33:24,120
Residents and oil field workers

430
00:33:24,121 --> 00:33:25,140
brought in heavy equipment.

431
00:33:26,280 --> 00:33:28,260
People was just coming from everywhere.

432
00:33:28,680 --> 00:33:30,120
They just started hollering

433
00:33:30,121 --> 00:33:32,200
and screaming. People everywhere just

434
00:33:32,201 --> 00:33:34,020
couldn't find their children.

435
00:33:34,021 --> 00:33:35,740
They carried them everywhere.

436
00:33:35,741 --> 00:33:38,400
they had to start making makeshift hospitals.

437
00:33:39,540 --> 00:33:42,900
Legions of volunteers and parents frantically dug through the night,

438
00:33:43,240 --> 00:33:44,340
looking for the young victims.

439
00:33:44,840 --> 00:33:48,360
Within 17 hours, it was cleaned up.

440
00:33:48,840 --> 00:33:52,060
They cleaned it up so fast that it hindered the investigation.

441
00:33:52,780 --> 00:33:55,660
By the time they got people in here to investigate what happened,

442
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:57,160
there was nothing to look at.

443
00:33:58,140 --> 00:34:00,380
The grief of the tragedy was widespread.

444
00:34:01,100 --> 00:34:02,980
Condolences came from all over the world,

445
00:34:02,981 --> 00:34:05,320
including a telegram from Eleanor Roosevelt.

446
00:34:05,740 --> 00:34:09,220
as well as one from the Chancellor of Germany, Adolf Hitler.

447
00:34:10,560 --> 00:34:13,260
We returned to school ten days later.

448
00:34:13,860 --> 00:34:19,120
Some of us, a lot of us wasn't there.

449
00:34:20,120 --> 00:34:24,760
They called a roll in the old gymnasium and gathered by grades,

450
00:34:24,761 --> 00:34:31,100
and there was probably 50% of the normal capacity of the classes there or less.

451
00:34:31,101 --> 00:34:39,120
And that's when I discovered that the girl I traded seats with was killed.

452
00:34:40,340 --> 00:34:44,880
And like I say, I carried that guilt with me.

453
00:34:45,600 --> 00:34:51,780
Of the estimated 650 students and teachers in the building, 319 were dead.

454
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:58,800
There were three investigating agencies came in, the State Board of Inquiry, the Bureau of Mines and Agricultural Department.

455
00:34:58,801 --> 00:35:04,680
They all reached the same conclusion that no one is to be held liable for the school explosion.

456
00:35:05,120 --> 00:35:10,700
Since natural gas at that time had no odor, it would not have mattered how they were getting it or how they were using it.

457
00:35:10,780 --> 00:35:11,560
It would still happen.

458
00:35:12,760 --> 00:35:17,860
Three days after the explosion, Texas legislature called a special session

459
00:35:17,861 --> 00:35:25,220
in order to pass the odorization law that we smell gas today for our safety and protection.

460
00:35:25,221 --> 00:35:29,220
The Texas regulation was quickly adopted worldwide.

461
00:35:29,221 --> 00:35:35,220
But tragically, it took the lives of nearly an entire generation in one small town

462
00:35:35,221 --> 00:35:39,220
to bring about this momentous leap forward in safety.

463
00:35:43,220 --> 00:35:46,220
The smell of concentrated mercaptan is almost unbearable.

464
00:35:46,221 --> 00:35:51,220
It takes only a few parts per million to give natural gas a distinct odor.

465
00:35:51,221 --> 00:36:00,104
Doter. Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

466
00:36:00,105 --> 00:36:04,320
We now return to Engineering

467
00:36:04,321 --> 00:36:06,542
Disasters on Modern Marvels.

468
00:36:06,543 --> 00:36:11,860
Since the inception of the oil tanker, many accidents have garnered

469
00:36:11,861 --> 00:36:13,093
horrific headlines.

470
00:36:13,094 --> 00:36:17,960
In the 1990s, there was on average more than one oil tanker spill every two

471
00:36:17,961 --> 00:36:18,661
weeks.

472
00:36:18,662 --> 00:36:24,280
But it was the catastrophic grounding of the Torrey Canyon oil tanker in 1967 that

473
00:36:24,281 --> 00:36:30,247
introduced the world to the devastation of the super spell.

474
00:36:30,248 --> 00:36:33,180
On March 18, 1967, the Torrey

475
00:36:33,181 --> 00:36:36,299
Canyon ran aground off the southern coast of England.

476
00:36:36,300 --> 00:36:38,300
Within two weeks, her entire cargo

477
00:36:38,301 --> 00:36:41,274
of 119,000 tons of crude oil was lost.

478
00:36:41,275 --> 00:36:45,500
Close to 200 miles of pristine coastline were defiled.

479
00:36:45,501 --> 00:36:49,480
This was a really big spill even by modern standards.

480
00:36:49,760 --> 00:36:54,620
The tanker spilled three times the oil that the Exxon Valdez spilled in Alaska,

481
00:36:55,000 --> 00:36:57,020
and we know what damage that spill caused.

482
00:36:57,980 --> 00:37:02,960
Nearly four decades later, the Torrey Canyon accident serves as a valuable lesson

483
00:37:02,961 --> 00:37:07,960
of how poorly designed instruments can be the catalyst for human error on a grand scale.

484
00:37:09,040 --> 00:37:13,000
In 1959, when the Torrey Canyon was first put into operation,

485
00:37:13,540 --> 00:37:16,440
she had a cargo capacity of 60,000 tons of oil,

486
00:37:17,080 --> 00:37:21,980
considered small in an era when many ships had a capacity of over 100,000 tons.

487
00:37:23,240 --> 00:37:26,200
In the late 60s, our dependence on oil,

488
00:37:26,340 --> 00:37:29,260
the consumption of oil worldwide was growing astronomically.

489
00:37:29,840 --> 00:37:32,320
Oil was about 30 cents a gallon in the U.S.,

490
00:37:32,321 --> 00:37:34,180
and cars got 10 miles to the gallon,

491
00:37:34,600 --> 00:37:37,000
so we were using more and more oil globally.

492
00:37:37,520 --> 00:37:40,160
Consequently, the rise of the supertanker,

493
00:37:40,161 --> 00:37:43,540
the transportation of oil started to take on new dimensions.

494
00:37:44,280 --> 00:37:47,360
In 1964, as oil demand increased,

495
00:37:47,860 --> 00:37:50,600
the owners of the Torrey Canyon decided she would be more profitable

496
00:37:50,601 --> 00:37:52,200
with an even larger capacity.

497
00:37:52,880 --> 00:37:57,180
In 1965, Torrey Canyon was brought to the Sasebo Shipbuilding Company

498
00:37:57,181 --> 00:37:58,520
in Japan for jumboization.

499
00:37:59,440 --> 00:38:02,000
Jumboization, in the case of the Torrey Canyon,

500
00:38:02,180 --> 00:38:05,800
as well as many other tankers, involved cutting it at midships,

501
00:38:06,280 --> 00:38:07,360
separating the two halves,

502
00:38:07,361 --> 00:38:11,600
and then inserting a large section in between with additional cargo tanks.

503
00:38:12,140 --> 00:38:14,920
When the Torrey Canyon went back to work in 1965,

504
00:38:15,560 --> 00:38:17,940
she was the 13th largest vessel in the world,

505
00:38:18,440 --> 00:38:21,920
capable of carrying close to 122,000 tons of oil.

506
00:38:22,880 --> 00:38:26,040
The ship was also equipped with an autopilot feature.

507
00:38:27,000 --> 00:38:30,040
Well over 90% of the time on the Torrey Canyon,

508
00:38:30,200 --> 00:38:33,820
as well as similar ships like it at the time, they were on autopilot.

509
00:38:33,821 --> 00:38:41,600
and the autopilot system allowed the helmsman to set a compass heading,

510
00:38:42,240 --> 00:38:46,560
such as 18 degrees, and it would just hold that compass heading.

511
00:38:47,080 --> 00:38:51,940
Several days before the fateful collision, the autopilot was set to 18 degrees north,

512
00:38:52,360 --> 00:38:55,140
sending the Torrey Canyon well clear of the Silly Isles,

513
00:38:55,460 --> 00:38:58,820
a chain of rocky islands which stood between the ship and her final destination,

514
00:38:59,980 --> 00:39:01,540
a port on the west coast of England.

515
00:39:01,541 --> 00:39:11,882
The captain had the ship running at full speed, 17 knots, in an effort to beat a narrow tidal window on the evening of March 18th, when high tides would make the port

516
00:39:11,883 --> 00:39:14,000
deep enough for the ship to enter.

517
00:39:14,600 --> 00:39:25,221
If he missed it, they would have to wait six days, and the costs for the crew and the ship and reserving the space in the harbor were substantial, so he was under

518
00:39:25,222 --> 00:39:28,040
considerable time stress to make this date.

519
00:39:28,920 --> 00:39:32,240
Before he turned in on what would be his last night afloat,

520
00:39:32,640 --> 00:39:36,040
the captain left orders to be called when the islands were detected on the radar,

521
00:39:36,620 --> 00:39:38,480
signifying their final approach to port.

522
00:39:39,320 --> 00:39:44,620
Early the next morning, radar revealed that strong currents had unexpectedly pushed the ship to the east.

523
00:39:45,320 --> 00:39:49,920
The Torrey Canyon was aiming right for a precarious channel between the islands and the coast.

524
00:39:50,540 --> 00:39:56,300
When he found that he was not where he thought he would be off the Silly Isles,

525
00:39:56,301 --> 00:40:04,440
his normal course would have been to steer all the way around to seaward of the isles and come to port.

526
00:40:05,520 --> 00:40:08,480
Going around the isles would add several hours to the journey,

527
00:40:09,500 --> 00:40:12,140
likely causing the tanker to miss high tide at the port.

528
00:40:13,040 --> 00:40:18,780
The captain decided that he would basically shoot the gap, about a six-mile gap,

529
00:40:19,240 --> 00:40:23,000
between the Silly Islands and the Seven Stone Shoals.

530
00:40:23,001 --> 00:40:30,600
It was a tricky maneuver and not something most any supertanker captain should seriously consider doing.

531
00:40:31,160 --> 00:40:37,100
But after entering the channel, the captain realized the autopilot had the tanker on a collision course

532
00:40:37,101 --> 00:40:39,740
with submerged shoals surrounding the islands.

533
00:40:40,220 --> 00:40:43,400
He shifted to manual steering and spun the wheel to the left,

534
00:40:43,800 --> 00:40:46,000
believing he was turning the ship away from the rocks.

535
00:40:46,520 --> 00:40:50,740
The fate of the ship was about to be sealed by a seemingly minor design flaw.

536
00:40:51,320 --> 00:40:54,600
The captain and the other officers went back to the chart room,

537
00:40:56,200 --> 00:40:59,700
thinking that they had just avoided a catastrophe,

538
00:40:59,980 --> 00:41:04,020
when he heard the helmsman screaming for him, saying that the ship was not responding.

539
00:41:04,021 --> 00:41:09,800
The ship was not responding because he had accidentally, unintentionally,

540
00:41:09,920 --> 00:41:16,200
pushed the control into the control mode, which disengaged the helm.

541
00:41:16,740 --> 00:41:18,060
The steering wheel was ineffective.

542
00:41:18,960 --> 00:41:22,960
After realizing the error, the captain moved the selector to manual steering.

543
00:41:22,961 --> 00:41:30,600
By then it was far too late, and the Torrey Canyon ground to a halt over Pollard Rock,

544
00:41:31,300 --> 00:41:35,500
part of the seven stone shoals, and was ripped wide open.

545
00:41:36,180 --> 00:41:40,840
The captain's inability to steer the ship out of harm's way was later attributed in part

546
00:41:40,841 --> 00:41:47,720
to the design of the autopilot mechanism, which slid easily and without warning from one gear to the next.

547
00:41:48,060 --> 00:41:53,900
The control position on the three-position lever was similar to neutral on a car.

548
00:41:54,700 --> 00:41:59,660
But unlike a car transmission system in which you go, say, from park to drive

549
00:41:59,661 --> 00:42:04,820
and you have to press a button or input, make a separate action to make that change,

550
00:42:04,821 --> 00:42:09,280
you could just shift this control to any of these three positions

551
00:42:09,281 --> 00:42:13,300
just by simply moving it intentionally or unintentionally.

552
00:42:13,301 --> 00:42:20,300
Within 72 hours of the collision, an estimated 37 million gallons of the tanker's cargo leaked out.

553
00:42:20,580 --> 00:42:31,180
The ship was gently creaking, and the oil was all around the ship from wherever she was ruptured in the hull.

554
00:42:31,740 --> 00:42:36,360
The tanker was so firmly impaled on the rocks that no tug could pull her off.

555
00:42:36,820 --> 00:42:39,400
Efforts to clean up the massive oil spill were futile.

556
00:42:40,100 --> 00:42:42,100
They went at this from every angle.

557
00:42:42,540 --> 00:42:44,440
They hit it with napalm.

558
00:42:44,580 --> 00:42:46,000
They tried to burn the oil away.

559
00:42:46,100 --> 00:42:48,080
They hit it with explosives trying to sink it.

560
00:42:48,180 --> 00:42:48,880
That didn't work.

561
00:42:49,180 --> 00:42:52,880
Then they hit it with tens of thousands of tons of detergent

562
00:42:52,881 --> 00:42:55,380
to try to soak the oil or disperse the oil.

563
00:42:55,720 --> 00:42:56,800
And that didn't help at all.

564
00:42:56,880 --> 00:42:59,040
In fact, that proved more toxic to the ecosystem.

565
00:42:59,440 --> 00:43:01,620
There was no precedent for this type of crash.

566
00:43:02,100 --> 00:43:05,820
Within a few years, more effective cleanup methods became common practice.

567
00:43:05,821 --> 00:43:10,540
But amazingly, a better autopilot design was not installed on oil tankers

568
00:43:10,541 --> 00:43:12,680
until over 20 years later.

569
00:43:13,200 --> 00:43:16,640
You had the same problem, it appears, on the Exxon Valdez

570
00:43:16,641 --> 00:43:18,420
with the autopilot engaged.

571
00:43:19,040 --> 00:43:22,700
They turned the wheel, but the vessel did not respond

572
00:43:22,701 --> 00:43:24,220
because it wasn't supposed to.

573
00:43:24,860 --> 00:43:27,640
After Exxon Valdez, they made a change

574
00:43:27,641 --> 00:43:31,880
so that if you turn the wheel while it is engaged,

575
00:43:32,620 --> 00:43:33,520
an alarm sounds.

576
00:43:34,800 --> 00:43:37,080
Navigational technology on oil tankers

577
00:43:37,081 --> 00:43:39,280
has been greatly improved since the Torrey Canyon.

578
00:43:40,260 --> 00:43:43,760
Nowadays, you can have alarms if you get off course, audible alarms,

579
00:43:44,540 --> 00:43:47,680
basically anything you want as far as the navigational aids.

580
00:43:48,080 --> 00:43:50,900
The GPS knows where the ship is on the surface of the Earth,

581
00:43:51,400 --> 00:43:56,700
and the rudder will be changed to keep the ship on the prescribed track

582
00:43:56,701 --> 00:43:58,360
that the master laid into the computer.

583
00:43:58,880 --> 00:44:02,580
The ever-increasing number of automated features on modern tankers

584
00:44:02,581 --> 00:44:05,780
are most successful when designed with the user in mind.

585
00:44:05,781 --> 00:44:12,780
Particularly today when systems are becoming so very, very reliable and well engineered,

586
00:44:12,781 --> 00:44:20,780
it is the user interface and the human factors that play the greatest role in the success

587
00:44:20,781 --> 00:44:24,780
or failure or reliability of major systems.

588
00:44:24,781 --> 00:44:31,780
Another lasting consequence of the ecological calamity was that the accident made the international community

589
00:44:31,781 --> 00:44:38,280
realized the need for stricter penalties for oil polluters.

590
00:44:38,281 --> 00:44:45,280
International policies were put into place to provide relief to the victims, and by 1969,

591
00:44:45,281 --> 00:44:48,480
ship owners became strictly liable for their spills.

